An object-oriented programming (OOP) language is a programming language that supports the concept of objects, which are instances of classes that encapsulate data and behavior. OOP languages allow you to create reusable code by defining classes and objects, and they provide mechanisms for inheritance, polymorphism, and encapsulation.

Some popular object-oriented programming languages include:

1. Java
2. C++
3. Python
4. Ruby
5. C#
6. Swift
7. Kotlin
8. Objective-C
9. PHP
10. JavaScript (with frameworks like React, Angular, and Vue)

In an OOP language, you can define a class that represents a real-world object, and then create instances of that class to represent specific objects. Each class can have properties (data) and methods (functions) that define the behavior of the object.

For example, in Java, you might define a `Car` class with properties like `make`, `model`, and `year`, and methods like `startEngine()` and `stopEngine()`:

```java
public class Car {
    private String make;
    private String model;
    private int year;

    public Car(String make, String model, int year) {
        this.make = make;
        this.model = model;
        this.year = year;
    }

    public void startEngine() {
        System.out.println("Starting engine...");
    }

    public void stopEngine() {
        System.out.println("Stopping engine...");
    }
}
```

You can then create instances of the `Car` class to represent specific cars:

```java
Car myCar = new Car("Toyota", "Corolla", 2022);
myCar.startEngine();
myCar.stopEngine();
```

In this example, `myCar` is an object of the `Car` class, and you can call its methods to interact with the car.

